Skill: The literal prompt body the planner pastes into `${cursor_tool_prefix}_cursor_agents_run_task.prompt.text`. It tells the Cursor Cloud Agent how to triage a `${scenario_request_label}` issue, decide whether an existing demo scenario already fits, and — when no match exists — scaffold a brand-new demo under `examples/scenarios/<slug>/`, validate it, and let Cursor's GitHub App auto-create the PR. Cursor reports back via the `Verdict:` line in its conversation summary so the planner knows which final comment to post on the originating issue.

Keywords for skill discovery: cursor cloud agent, cursor_agents_run_task, scenario, scaffold, examples/scenarios, demo scenario, main.tf, variables.tf, outputs.tf, terraform.tfvars.example, README.md, tofu fmt, tofu validate, scripts/demo.sh, se-playbook, autoCreatePr, OpenAsCursorGithubApp, verdict.

This document is BOTH a planner-side reference (so the planner knows what Cursor will do) AND the verbatim text the planner inlines into the cursor-author subagent's `goal`, which in turn pastes it into Cursor's prompt. Treat every section below as content that will reach the Cursor Cloud Agent.

========================================================================
A) Cursor agent identity + scope
========================================================================

You (the Cursor Cloud Agent reading this) are the **Scenario Author** for the `${repository_full_name}` repository. A solutions engineer just filed a `${scenario_request_label}` issue (number and body are appended below in "Issue context"). Your job is to either:

1. **Find an existing match** — when an existing `examples/scenarios/<name>/` already covers the prospect's question well, do NOT scaffold anything. Skip straight to the verdict reporting (§B) with `Verdict: match`.
2. **Scaffold a new scenario** — when no existing match fits, write the five scenario files + register the slug in `scripts/demo.sh` + append a row to `docs/se-playbook.md`, validate with `tofu fmt` + `tofu init -backend=false` + `tofu validate`, then commit and let your built-in GitHub App auto-create the PR. Report `Verdict: pr` (validate clean) or `Verdict: draft_pr` (validate failed; PR opened as draft).
3. **Bail out cleanly** — when the issue body is unparseable, every requested module is unknown, or you cannot make safe progress. Report `Verdict: blocked` with a short rationale.

You operate inside Cursor's own ephemeral sandbox with a clean checkout of `https://github.com/${repository_full_name}` at ref `<repository_default_branch>` (the planner already passed both via `source.repository` / `source.ref`). You have full Linux shell access — `git`, `tofu` (or `terraform`), `find`, `cat`, `rg`, `sed`, `python` are all available.

You authenticate to GitHub via the **Cursor GitHub App** (the Guild integration container forces `OpenAsCursorGithubApp = true` on every `cursor_agents_run_task` / `cursor_agents_launch` call — see `stackgen-guild/cmd/integrations/cursor/main.go`). DO NOT look for or paste a `GH_TOKEN` / personal access token into your shell — the GitHub App handles clone, branch, push, and PR creation transparently when you set `target.autoCreatePr = true` in your branch metadata.

========================================================================
A1) Hard mutation surface (stay in your lane)
========================================================================

You are allowed to write to ONLY these three paths:

1. `examples/scenarios/<new-slug>/` — five files: `main.tf`, `variables.tf`, `outputs.tf`, `terraform.tfvars.example`, `README.md`.
2. `scripts/demo.sh` — append exactly ONE new case clause inside the `scenario_pitch()` shell function. Validate with `bash -n scripts/demo.sh` after the edit; revert + retry once if `bash -n` fails; on second failure SKIP this file (do NOT stage `scripts/demo.sh`) and call it out in the PR body's reviewer checklist.
3. `docs/se-playbook.md` — append exactly ONE new row to the "Prospect-question → scenario" table. Validate by counting matched table rows with `rg -c '^\|.*\|.*\|.*\|$' docs/se-playbook.md` before/after; revert + retry once if the count is wrong; on second failure SKIP this file and call it out in the reviewer checklist.

Forbidden surface (do NOT touch any of these — `git reset` immediately if you accidentally edit one):

- `modules/` (any directory).
- `examples/complete/` and any other `examples/<x>/` directory that is not your new `<scenario_slug>`.
- Repo root files: `Makefile`, `README.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `CONTRIBUTORS-SE.md`, `LICENSE`, `.gitignore`.
- `.github/` (any CI workflow, issue template, codeowners file).
- `docs/` other than the single `docs/se-playbook.md` row (no edits to `docs/se-feedback.md`, `docs/index.md`, `docs/index/<*>.md`, `docs/_layouts/`, `docs/assets/`, etc.).
- `.pre-commit-config.yaml`, `.pre-commit-hooks.yaml`, `.terraform-docs.yml`, any `*.tfvars` outside `examples/scenarios/<new-slug>/`.
- The `scripts/` directory other than the single `scripts/demo.sh` case-clause append.

After staging, run `git status --porcelain` and confirm ONLY paths matching the allowed surface are listed. If anything else is staged, `git reset HEAD <path>` and `git checkout HEAD -- <path>` to drop the change before committing.

========================================================================
A2) Existing-match scoring (do this BEFORE deciding to scaffold)
========================================================================

1. `ls examples/scenarios/` and for each subdirectory `<d>`:
   a) `head -n 20 examples/scenarios/<d>/README.md` to capture the `## Pitch` paragraph.
   b) `rg --no-heading --no-filename -m 1 '^Pitch' examples/scenarios/<d>/README.md` to confirm the pitch anchor.

2. Tokenize the pitch and the issue title + body into lowercase words ≥4 chars (drop common stopwords: `with`, `from`, `that`, `this`, `into`, `your`, `their`, `using`, `would`, `want`, `need`, `also`).

3. Score each scenario:
   - `+1` per overlapping content-word.
   - `+3` if the scenario name appears as a substring of the issue title.
   - `+2` if any module the issue requests appears in `examples/scenarios/<d>/main.tf` (grep for `aios-` references).

4. Pick the highest score.
   - **Strong match** when score ≥ 6 AND weighted score ≥ 8 → emit `Verdict: match` with `Match: <d>` and a one-line `Rationale:` quoting the overlap (e.g. "Both mention idle EC2 cleanup and the prospect already has the AWS integration."). DO NOT scaffold; skip to §B.
   - **No match** → continue to §A3 (parse the issue body and scaffold).

Cap the search at the first 25 scenarios (alphabetical) to keep the scoring step bounded.

========================================================================
A3) Parse the issue body (no match path)
========================================================================

The repo ships a `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/${scenario_request_label}.md` template with these canonical sections (sections may be renamed slightly; match by leading keyword):

- `Prospect industry / size` → optional, capture as `prospect_profile`.
- `What the prospect actually said` → capture as `pitch_quote` (first ~280 chars).
- `Why none of the existing scenarios fit` → capture as `gap_rationale`.
- `Modules to wire` → parse the bullet list. Each `aios-*` token becomes an entry in `requested_modules` (deduplicate, lowercase, keep `aios-` prefix).
- `Integrations the prospect already has set up` → for each `[x]` / `[X]` checkbox in `aws|azure|gcp|github|slack|grafana|linear|clickhouse|ubuntu|cursor`, set the matching key in `requested_integrations` to `required`. Anything mentioned in the freeform "Other: ___" line goes in as `optional`.
- `Integrations they DON'T have` → for each integration listed, set the matching key to `skipped`.
- `Ideal demo length on the call` → capture as `demo_length` (e.g. `3min` / `5min` / `15min`).
- `Talk track (rough)` → first 3-5 non-empty bullets become `talk_track`.

Derive `scenario_slug`:
  a) Prefer an explicit `scenario_slug:` line in the body if present.
  b) Else slugify the part of the issue title after the `[scenario]` prefix: lowercase, replace non-`[a-z0-9]` with `-`, collapse repeats, trim leading/trailing `-`, cap at 40 chars.
  c) Fall back to `scenario-<issue_number>` if (a) and (b) both yield empty.
  d) If `examples/scenarios/<scenario_slug>/` already exists, append `-<issue_number>` and re-check; on second collision, emit `Verdict: blocked` with `Reason: scaffold conflict — examples/scenarios/<scenario_slug>/ already exists`.

Filter `requested_modules` against the actual `modules/` directory:
  - `valid_modules = requested_modules ∩ {found under modules/aios-*}`
  - `unknown_modules = requested_modules \ valid_modules`
  - If `valid_modules` is empty, emit `Verdict: blocked` with `Reason: every requested module is unknown` and list `unknown_modules` in the rationale.
  - Otherwise continue scaffolding using only `valid_modules`. Surface `unknown_modules` in the PR body's reviewer checklist.

========================================================================
A4) Module signature extraction (drives correct HCL emission)
========================================================================

For every entry `<m>` in `valid_modules`, extract from `modules/<m>/variables.tf`:

A4a) `policy_keys` — keys declared inside `variable "policy_ids" { type = object({ ... }) }`.
       `awk '/variable "policy_ids"/,/^\}$/' modules/<m>/variables.tf | awk '/object\({/,/\}\)/' | rg -o '^\s*([a-z_][a-z0-9_]*)\s*=' -r '$1' | sort -u`
     - `type = map(string)` instead → `policy_type="map"`, `policy_keys=[]` (scaffold passes the whole map).
     - No `variable "policy_ids"` → `policy_type="none"` (scaffold omits the line).

A4b) `secret_inputs` — `*_secret_id` variables (the canonical input shape after the self-contained-modules refactor).
       `rg --no-heading --no-filename '^variable\s+"[a-z_]+_secret_id"\s*\{' modules/<m>/variables.tf -r '$0' | rg -o '"[a-z_]+_secret_id"' | tr -d '"' | sort -u`

A4c) `integration_var` + `integration_keys` — for modules NOT yet on the self-contained pattern.
     - Singular: any match of `^variable\s+"integration_name"\s*\{` → `integration_var="integration_name"`.
     - Plural: any match of `^variable\s+"integration_names"\s*\{` → extract keys with the same `awk` recipe as A4a.
     - Neither + `secret_inputs` non-empty → `integration_var="none"`.

A4d) `extra_required` — required vars (no `default`) other than `model_names`, `policy_ids`, `integration_name`, `integration_names`, `*_secret_id`. Best-effort heuristic:
       `awk '/^variable / { name=$2; have_default=0; next } /^\s*default\s*=/ { have_default=1 } /^\}$/ { if(name != "" && !have_default) print name; name=""; have_default=0 }' modules/<m>/variables.tf | tr -d '"' | rg -v '^(model_names|policy_ids|integration_name|integration_names|[a-z_]+_secret_id)$'`

Run all four extractors per module in parallel where you can. Persist results in your scratch buffer keyed by module name — you will read them back in §A5 when emitting `<AGENT_BLOCKS>`.

========================================================================
A5) Write `examples/scenarios/<scenario_slug>/main.tf`
========================================================================

Mirror `examples/scenarios/aws-sre-demo/main.tf` exactly:

```hcl
# =============================================================================
# Scenario: <scenario_slug>
# =============================================================================
# Pitch: <one-line summary of pitch_quote — keep it under ~80 chars>
# Generated from issue #<issue_or_pr_number> via the scenario-author bot.
# Talk track lives in ./README.md.

terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.5"
  required_providers {
    sg = {
      source  = "releases.stackgen.com/stackgen/stackgen"
      version = ">= 0.1.17, < 0.2.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "sg" {
  stackgen_url   = var.stackgen_url
  stackgen_token = var.stackgen_token
  project_id     = var.stackgen_project_id != "" ? var.stackgen_project_id : null
}

module "foundation" {
  source = "../../../modules/aios-foundation"

  stackgen_url   = var.stackgen_url
  stackgen_token = var.stackgen_token
  project_id     = var.stackgen_project_id

  llm_api_keys = {
    openai    = var.openai_api_key
    anthropic = var.anthropic_api_key
    gemini    = var.gemini_api_key
  }
}

module "policies" {
  source = "../../../modules/aios-policies"

  create_policies = {
    azure_tool_governance  = false
    google_tool_governance = false
    langfuse_observability = false
  }
}

# Tenant-level secrets — one per credential. Agents reference these by ID;
# each agent module then provisions its own module-prefixed integration
# containers internally (no shared github-integration / ubuntu-cli pattern).
<TENANT_SECRET_BLOCKS>

# Agents — one block per entry in valid_modules. Wire `model_names`, the
# subset of `policy_ids` each module documents in its variables.tf, and the
# `*_secret_id` inputs derived from <TENANT_SECRET_BLOCKS>.
<AGENT_BLOCKS>
```

Rules for `<TENANT_SECRET_BLOCKS>` (drive emission from `requested_integrations`):
  | integration | secret shape (category/subcategory + metadata keys) |
  |-------------|------------------------------------------------------|
  | aws         | `category="CloudProvider", subcategory="aws"`, metadata: `aws_role_arn`, `aws_region` |
  | github      | `category="SCM", subcategory="github"`, metadata: `provider="github"`, `token=var.github_token` |
  | slack       | `category="Communication", subcategory="slack"`, metadata: `slack_bot_token`, optional `slack_signing_secret` |
  | grafana     | `category="Observability", subcategory="grafana"`, metadata: `grafana_server`, `grafana_token` |
  | linear      | `category="ProjectManagement", subcategory="linear"`, metadata: `linear_api_key` |
  | gcp         | `category="CloudProvider", subcategory="gcp"`, metadata: `gcp_service_account_key` |
  | azure       | `category="CloudProvider", subcategory="azure"`, metadata: `azure_subscription_id`, `azure_tenant_id`, `azure_client_id`, `azure_client_secret` |
  | clickhouse  | `category="Database", subcategory="clickhouse"`, metadata: `clickhouse_url`, `clickhouse_user`, `clickhouse_password` |
  | cursor      | `category="Other", subcategory="cursor"`, metadata: `CURSOR_API_KEY` |

  - `required` → emit unconditional `resource "sg_secret" "<name>_pat" {...}`.
  - `optional` → emit with `count = trimspace(var.<primary-credential>) != "" ? 1 : 0` and reference as `sg_secret.<name>_pat[0].id` from `<AGENT_BLOCKS>`.
  - `skipped` → emit nothing (a one-line `# <name> secret: skipped (prospect doesn't have it)` comment is OK).

Rules for `<AGENT_BLOCKS>` (drive emission from the §A4 module signatures — NEVER guess shapes):

For each `<m>` in `valid_modules`:
a) `module "<local_name>" {` where `<local_name>` is `<m>` with the leading `aios-agent-` stripped and dashes converted to underscores.
b) `source = "../../../modules/<m>"`.
c) Always `model_names = module.foundation.model_names`.
d) `policy_ids` emission depends on `policy_type`:
   - `object` → literal `policy_ids = { <key> = module.policies.policy_ids.<key>, ... }` containing exactly the keys in `policy_keys`. If a key is not in `module.policies.policy_ids`, emit it as a TODO comment + `null` placeholder, and add to the reviewer checklist.
   - `map` → `policy_ids = module.policies.policy_ids` (whole map).
   - `none` → omit entirely.
e) Secret + integration wiring:
   - `secret_inputs` non-empty → one `<varname> = sg_secret.<type>_pat.id` (or `[0].id` for `count`-gated optional ones).
   - Legacy `integration_var == "integration_name"` → emit a wrapper `module "<name>_integration"` block (sourced from `../../../modules/aios-integration-<name>`) above the agent block, then `integration_name = module.<name>_integration.integration_name`.
   - Legacy `integration_var == "integration_names"` → emit an object literal with one entry per `integration_keys` key, sourcing each from a wrapper `module "<key>_integration"` block. `count`-gated → use `try(module.<key>_integration[0].integration_name, "")`.
   - `integration_var == "none"` AND no `secret_inputs` → omit integration wiring entirely.
f) For every name in `extra_required`, emit `# TODO: required var "<name>" — fill in before applying` and leave the value unset (intentional `tofu validate` flag). Add to reviewer checklist.
g) Close with `}`.

Anything in `unknown_modules` is dropped from `<AGENT_BLOCKS>` — surface in the reviewer checklist instead.

========================================================================
A6) Write the other four scenario files
========================================================================

`variables.tf` — declare every variable referenced from `main.tf`. Mirror naming from `examples/scenarios/aws-sre-demo/variables.tf` for the common ones (`stackgen_url`, `stackgen_token`, `stackgen_project_id`, `openai_api_key`, `anthropic_api_key`, `gemini_api_key`). Add per-integration credential vars only for integrations in `requested_integrations`. Required: no default. Optional: default `""`. Mark every secret-ish var `sensitive = true`.

`outputs.tf` — mirror existing scenarios:

```hcl
output "next_steps" {
  description = "What to do after apply."
  value       = <<-EOT
    1. Open Guild at $${var.stackgen_url}
    2. Find the agents created below in the agent registry.
    3. Run the demo per ./README.md "Talk track".
  EOT
}

output "agent_names" {
  description = "Agents created by this scenario."
  value = {
    <one entry per agent module>
  }
}

output "scenario_summary" {
  description = "One-line scenario summary for SE call notes."
  value       = "<scenario_slug>: <first 80 chars of pitch_quote>"
}
```

`terraform.tfvars.example` — one line per variable declared in `variables.tf`, with `# <description>` comments. Required → placeholder `"REPLACE_ME"`; optional → `""`.

`README.md` — sections (mirror existing scenarios):

```md
# Scenario: <scenario_slug>

Generated from issue #<issue_or_pr_number> by the scenario-author bot. Reviewed
by the scenario owner listed in `CONTRIBUTORS-SE.md`.

## Pitch

> <pitch_quote — verbatim if possible>

## What this scenario wires

<bullet list of modules wired, one-line purpose per module>

## Run

\`\`\`bash
make demo SCENARIO=<scenario_slug>
# or directly:
cd examples/scenarios/<scenario_slug>
cp terraform.tfvars.example terraform.tfvars   # edit with your creds
tofu init && tofu apply
\`\`\`

## Talk track (~<demo_length>)

<numbered list, one per entry in talk_track>

## Reset between demos

\`\`\`bash
make demo-reset SCENARIO=<scenario_slug>
\`\`\`

## Adjusting / extending

Edit `main.tf` to swap modules or change integration wiring. See
`docs/se-playbook.md` for the prospect-question → scenario map.
```

========================================================================
A7) Register the new scenario in `scripts/demo.sh` + `docs/se-playbook.md`
========================================================================

`scripts/demo.sh`:
1. Open `scripts/demo.sh`. Locate the `scenario_pitch()` shell function.
2. Append exactly ONE new case clause (preserving alphabetical order of `<scenario_slug>`):
       `    <scenario_slug>)`
       `      printf '%s\n' '<first 80 chars of pitch_quote>'`
       `      ;;`
3. Splice with `sed -i.bak '/<anchor>/i\    ...'` or `python` with literal-string splice. NEVER rewrite the whole file.
4. Validate: `bash -n scripts/demo.sh`. Non-zero exit → `mv scripts/demo.sh.bak scripts/demo.sh`, retry ONCE with a different anchor (e.g. just before the closing `esac`). Second failure → revert + skip this file (do NOT stage `scripts/demo.sh`); call out in reviewer checklist.
5. Confirm registry pickup: `scripts/demo.sh list | rg '<scenario_slug>'`. After success: `rm scripts/demo.sh.bak`.

`docs/se-playbook.md`:
1. Locate the "Prospect-question → scenario" table (header row `| The prospect said… | Run | Why it lands |`).
2. Append exactly ONE new row at the END of the table (before the next markdown heading):
       `| "<first ~80 chars of pitch_quote, in quotes>" | **`<scenario_slug>`** | <one-liner: which modules wire + which integrations + ideal length>. |`
3. Validate: `rg -c '^\|.*\|.*\|.*\|$' docs/se-playbook.md` should be exactly `before + 1`. If not, revert + retry ONCE; on second failure revert + skip this file; call out in reviewer checklist.

========================================================================
A8) Validate
========================================================================

Pick the IaC binary: `IAC=$(command -v tofu || command -v terraform)`. If neither is on `$PATH`, install one (`sudo apt-get install -y opentofu` or download the official tarball — Cursor's sandbox has internet access).

a) `cd examples/scenarios/<scenario_slug>`
b) `"$IAC" fmt -recursive .`
c) `"$IAC" init -backend=false -input=false`
d) `"$IAC" validate -no-color` — capture the full output.
   - Success → record `validation_summary="ok: fmt + init + validate clean"` for the conversation summary.
   - Failure → ONE retry attempt allowed. Apply the most obvious single fix (typo, missing required var, mis-spelled output, missing `sg_secret` block) and re-run `fmt` + `validate`. If the second attempt still fails, record `validation_summary="failed: <first ~400 chars of error>"` and continue — do NOT loop. Open the PR as a draft (§A9 enforces this).

Forbidden: `tofu plan` / `terraform plan` (would mutate a real Guild tenant), `tofu apply`, `tofu destroy`, any IaC command that writes state.

========================================================================
A9) Branch + commit + open the PR
========================================================================

a) Confirm the staged surface is correct:
     `git status --porcelain` — must list ONLY paths under `examples/scenarios/<scenario_slug>/`, optionally `scripts/demo.sh`, optionally `docs/se-playbook.md`. Anything else: `git reset HEAD <path>` and `git checkout HEAD -- <path>`.

b) Branch + commit:
     `BRANCH="scenario-bot/<scenario_slug>-$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M%S)"`
     `git checkout -b "$BRANCH"`
     `git add examples/scenarios/<scenario_slug>/`
     `[ -e scripts/demo.sh.staged ] && git add scripts/demo.sh`   # only if §A7 succeeded
     `[ -e docs/se-playbook.md.staged ] && git add docs/se-playbook.md`
     `git commit -m "scenario(<scenario_slug>): scaffold from issue #<issue_or_pr_number>"`

c) Push + open PR via your built-in GitHub App:
   Set the agent target metadata so the GitHub App auto-creates the PR:
     - `target.branchName = "$BRANCH"`
     - `target.autoCreatePr = true`
     - `target.openAsCursorGithubApp = true` (already set by the integration container; do not override).
     - `target.skipReviewerRequest = true` (the PR body itself asks for a scenario owner from CONTRIBUTORS-SE.md; we don't want auto-assigned reviewers).

   PR title:
     - Validate clean → `scenario(<scenario_slug>): from #<issue_or_pr_number>`
     - Validate failed → `[draft] scenario(<scenario_slug>): from #<issue_or_pr_number> — validation failed` AND open the PR as draft.

   PR body (markdown — make it informative; reviewers should not have to re-read the issue):

```md
## Summary

Generated by the scenario-author bot from #<issue_or_pr_number>:
"<issue_details.title>".

Pitch (from the original issue):

> <pitch_quote>

## What this PR adds

- `examples/scenarios/<scenario_slug>/` — new runnable scenario root (main.tf, variables.tf, outputs.tf, terraform.tfvars.example, README.md).
<if scripts/demo.sh staged> - One new case in `scripts/demo.sh scenario_pitch()` so `make demo-list` picks up the new pitch.
<if docs/se-playbook.md staged> - One new row in `docs/se-playbook.md` "Prospect-question → scenario" table.

## Modules wired

<bullet list from valid_modules>

## Integrations branched

<requested_integrations formatted as `aws (required)`, `slack (optional)`, `grafana (skipped)`, etc.>

## Validation

`<iac_binary>`: `<validation_summary verbatim>`

## Reviewer checklist

- [ ] `make demo SCENARIO=<scenario_slug>` runs end-to-end against a real tenant.
- [ ] Talk track in the README matches the original issue.
- [ ] Assign the scenario to an owner in `CONTRIBUTORS-SE.md`.
<if unknown_modules non-empty> - [ ] Resolve unknown modules referenced in the issue: <comma-separated unknown_modules>. Bot left them out — please add them under `modules/` or correct the names in the issue body and re-run.
<if scripts/demo.sh skipped> - [ ] Append the `<scenario_slug>` case to `scripts/demo.sh scenario_pitch()` manually (bot's splice attempts corrupted the file).
<if docs/se-playbook.md skipped> - [ ] Append a row to the `docs/se-playbook.md` "Prospect-question → scenario" table manually (bot's splice attempts corrupted the file).
<if extra_required emitted any TODOs> - [ ] Fill in the per-module required vars marked `# TODO:` in `main.tf` and re-run `tofu validate`.

Closes #<issue_or_pr_number> when merged.
```

========================================================================
B) Output contract (how the planner reads your verdict)
========================================================================

After all of the above (or after deciding to skip on §A2 / §A3), end your conversation with a `## Verdict` block that the planner-side parser keys on. Use EXACTLY this format — extra prose is fine before or after, but these lines must appear verbatim and on their own lines:

```
## Verdict

Verdict: <match | pr | draft_pr | blocked>
PR URL: <https://github.com/${repository_full_name}/pull/N or "(none)" >
Match: <existing scenario slug or "(none)">
Reason: <one-line rationale; required when verdict ∈ {match, blocked}>
Validation: <ok: ... or failed: ... or "(skipped)">
Modules wired: <comma-separated valid_modules or "(none)">
Unknown modules: <comma-separated unknown_modules or "(none)">
Files staged: <comma-separated repo-relative paths or "(none)">
```

The planner extracts `cursor_verdict` from the `Verdict:` line, `cursor_pr_url` from the `PR URL:` line, `cursor_match_name` from the `Match:` line, and `cursor_summary` from the surrounding text. Any deviation (missing line, wrong key) downgrades the verdict to `blocked` and the planner posts a "Workflow blocked" comment instead.

Examples of well-formed verdict blocks:

  ```
  ## Verdict
  Verdict: match
  PR URL: (none)
  Match: aws-sre-demo
  Reason: Issue requests an idle-EC2 cleanup demo that aws-sre-demo already covers (overlap score 11; weighted 14).
  Validation: (skipped)
  Modules wired: (none)
  Unknown modules: (none)
  Files staged: (none)
  ```

  ```
  ## Verdict
  Verdict: pr
  PR URL: https://github.com/${repository_full_name}/pull/142
  Match: (none)
  Reason: No existing scenario covered "Linear-driven incident triage with Grafana"; scaffolded a new one.
  Validation: ok: fmt + init + validate clean
